  "text": "Question six. How do you paginate a large collection? The simple way is offset and limit: skip forty, take twenty. It can jump to any page, but the database walks every row it skips. Run it in Postgres, the open-source relational database, on a hundred thousand articles, newest first. Page one reads twenty rows. Page five hundred reads ten thousand to return twenty, and page five thousand reads all hundred thousand. It can also repeat rows. A reader takes page one, a new article is published, and page two starts with the article page one ended on. Cursor pagination hands back an opaque cursor marking where the page stopped, here the last article's ID. The next request asks for what comes after it: twenty rows read at any depth, and nothing shifts when articles arrive. It costs two things: no jumping straight to page five hundred, and an order that's unique and never changes, so a sort by time adds the ID as a tiebreaker.",
